UPDATE companies SET description='Boxcar Woody operates from Stockyards City in Oklahoma City, Oklahoma. The business traces its roots to 2015, when the founders of Boxcar Revival embarked on a road trip to salvage materials from retired American boxcars. That experience inspired the creation of Boxcar Woody, a venture dedicated to reclaiming authentic freight lumber and giving it new life in homes and businesses nationwide.

The company selects, mills, and finishes reclaimed boxcar and cargo wood, as well as whiskey barrel staves, in its Oklahoma workshop. From these materials it produces raw lumber, countertops, mantels, and a range of finished goods including dining tables, chairs, barstools, lighting, barn doors, and accessories. Each piece is handcrafted and can be made to order in custom sizes, with no two items exactly alike.

Its reclaimed wood has been used for kitchen countertops, island tops, bathroom vanities, sliding barn doors, bar tops with waterfall edges, and exterior tables. The company also offers a line of whiskey barrel products such as chairs, barrels, and serving trays.

The founders started Boxcar Woody to preserve the legacy of the American railroads by turning historic materials into functional, lasting pieces. They emphasize quality craftsmanship and the unique character that comes from decades of service on the rails, positioning the reclaimed lumber as a connection to the nation’s transportation history.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Reclaimed wood selection and milling", "Custom furniture design and fabrication", "Countertop fabrication", "Mantel fabrication", "Lighting fabrication", "Whiskey barrel product fabrication", "Barn door fabrication", "Finishing and staining", "Installation referral"], "industries": ["Residential", "Commercial"], "materials": ["Reclaimed boxcar lumber", "Reclaimed cargo lumber", "Whiskey barrel staves", "Wormy maple", "Douglas fir", "Cotton"], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Authentic reclaimed boxcar and cargo lumber sourced from retired American freight cars", "Every piece handcrafted in Stockyards City, Oklahoma", "One-of-one furniture: no two tables are exactly alike", "Made-to-order custom sizing available", "Whiskey barrel product line using reclaimed barrel staves"], "facts_used": ["Location: Oklahoma City, Oklahoma (Stockyards City) -> /", "Founding: Boxcar Revival began in 2015, leading to Boxcar Woody -> /", "Process: Selecting, milling, finishing reclaimed boxcar and cargo wood in workshop -> /", "Products: Raw lumber, countertops, mantels, finished furniture (tables, chairs, barstools, lighting, barn doors, accessories), whiskey barrel products -> /", "Industries served: Residential and commercial customers (homeowners, designers, contractors) -> /", "Differentiators: Authentic reclaimed boxcar lumber, one-of-one pieces, handcrafted in Oklahoma, made-to-order, preserving railroad history -> /", "Materials: Reclaimed boxcar lumber, cargo lumber, whiskey barrel staves, wormy maple, Douglas fir, cotton -> /", "Company type: Manufacturer (handcrafted products) -> /"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "By carefully selecting, milling, and finishing each piece in our Stockyards City, Oklahoma workshop, we preserve the integrity of the original material while crafting products designed for modern spaces.", "founded_year": "", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='boxcar-woody-reclaimed-lumber' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Boyko Inc. was founded in August 1987 in Stanton, North Dakota, by brothers Ron and Tom Boyko as a USDA‑inspected food manufacturing plant. The family‑owned corporation initially produced bakery and meat products before expanding into additional food items.

After two decades of manufacturing, the company sold its production operations in 2006 to focus on the used equipment market. Since 1996 Boyko has bought and sold used food‑processing machinery, and by 2002 it was doing so full‑time, sourcing equipment at auctions nationwide and partnering with auction firm Schneider Industries. Today the firm operates as a distributor and service provider, offering equipment listings, consignment sales, liquidations, asset‑management programs, technical support, and process‑plant consulting for food‑processing facilities.

The company serves food‑manufacturing customers across the processing industry, helping them acquire or dispose of equipment such as mixers, ovens, conveyors, packaging lines, and freezing systems. Its client base includes plants seeking individual machines or complete production lines, though no specific customers are named in public materials.

